WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:03.310
In this video, we're going to take a look

2
00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:06.070
at Nanite and virtual shadow maps.

3
00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:09.349
And essentially, we'll be asking why we should use Nanite,

4
00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:11.589
especially when in conjunction with Lumen.

5
00:00:11.589 --> 00:00:13.949
We'll be taking a look at how you actually can use it

6
00:00:13.949 --> 00:00:15.750
and how to set it up, and then

7
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.510
just have a quick overview of what virtual shadow maps are,

8
00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:22.559
what the limitations are, and how they actually work.

9
00:00:22.559 --> 00:00:23.839
So why Nanite?

10
00:00:23.839 --> 00:00:28.120
It's Unreal Engine 5's virtualized geometry system.

11
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.719
It's still this hierarchical system of smaller parts

12
00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:33.479
used in triangles and allows you to have

13
00:00:33.479 --> 00:00:37.270
a lot more geometric complexity with fewer issues.

14
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.630
It allows for a higher visual fidelity

15
00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.590
and works really well with Lumen.

16
00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:43.429
Outside of Lumen, the generating of shadow maps

17
00:00:43.429 --> 00:00:47.950
will also take too long on a complicated mesh.

18
00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:50.770
So being used in conjunction with Lumen,

19
00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:53.509
you'll get much better results.

20
00:00:53.509 --> 00:00:56.829
Virtual shadow maps are used for more realistic results.

21
00:00:56.829 --> 00:00:58.429
And instant object count and draw calls

22
00:00:58.429 --> 00:00:59.350
are less of a limitation.

23
00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.590
You can have each instance now with millions and millions

24
00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:05.469
of polygons, and you get automatic LODs,

25
00:01:05.469 --> 00:01:08.150
which use a cluster of data.

26
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.750
Importantly, it's more visually

27
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.510
accurate than displacement.

28
00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:16.500
You get fewer shadow artifacts than using

29
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.939
an old system like in Unreal Engine 4's material

30
00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:20.510
displacement.

31
00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.390
It's inaccurate, especially with extreme shadowing changes

32
00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:26.099
and light changes.

33
00:01:26.099 --> 00:01:28.560
And the good thing is, again, that Nanite is great for really

34
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.640
high-resolution models, and you keep really good accuracy

35
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.540
in the shadow and through the virtual shadow maps.

36
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.859
So to enable Nanite on a mesh, it's really, really

37
00:01:37.859 --> 00:01:39.099
quite simple.

38
00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:41.379
You can open up the Static Mesh Editor

39
00:01:41.379 --> 00:01:44.140
and tick the Build Nanite option here,

40
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.700
and it will be built automatically for you.

41
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.900
You can also right-click on the asset, go to Nanite,

42
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.930
and enable.

43
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.810
And if you're using Chaos, you

44
00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.010
can also change it in the Collections Editor.

45
00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:00.780
So you can have Enable Nanite support right there.

46
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.459
One note, actually, is that virtual textures aren't required

47
00:02:03.459 --> 00:02:06.700
to be used with Nanite, but they are highly recommended,

48
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.219
especially if you're making a large world.

49
00:02:09.219 --> 00:02:12.020
And we'll go into it a little bit later in a second,

50
00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.020
but it essentially allows the MIP generation

51
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.729
to be a little bit more performant.

52
00:02:18.729 --> 00:02:20.169
So when it comes to Nanite, you

53
00:02:20.169 --> 00:02:22.370
can change some of the Nanite properties

54
00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.590
via the Tools pulldown.

55
00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.009
And one important thing as well

56
00:02:26.009 --> 00:02:28.669
is that Nanite doesn't support hollow geometry,

57
00:02:28.669 --> 00:02:31.310
so non-watertight meshes.

58
00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:32.629
And really, it does support it.

59
00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:34.189
It just means that the shadowing from those

60
00:02:34.189 --> 00:02:35.189
will be inaccurate.

61
00:02:35.189 --> 00:02:37.229
So you could use blocking geometry,

62
00:02:37.229 --> 00:02:39.110
like a cube, to go behind that section

63
00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:43.539
to actually get the shadowing that you need.

64
00:02:43.539 --> 00:02:46.620
So let's take a look at virtual shadow maps.

65
00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.539
The important thing about virtual shadow maps,

66
00:02:48.539 --> 00:02:50.379
more than anything, is that it significantly

67
00:02:50.379 --> 00:02:53.580
increases the resolution and makes sure that it

68
00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.979
matches the Nanite geometry.

69
00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.979
And this is different to just normal shadow maps,

70
00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:03.129
which is just essentially a texture projection.

71
00:03:03.129 --> 00:03:06.569
You get plausible soft shadows with really,

72
00:03:06.569 --> 00:03:09.569
it says reasonable here, really nice, actually,

73
00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:11.969
in control of performance costs.

74
00:03:11.969 --> 00:03:14.729
Provides a simple solution that works by default

75
00:03:14.729 --> 00:03:17.460
with limited amounts of adjustment.

76
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.699
And it replaces the use of stationary lights

77
00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.939
in many cases and shadowing techniques

78
00:03:21.939 --> 00:03:25.379
with a single unified path of just using those movable lights.

79
00:03:25.379 --> 00:03:27.099
It's really made for Lumen.

80
00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:30.400
It's not intended for really anything else.

81
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.560
So that's something to keep in mind.

82
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.360
Compared to big shadow maps, the things it solves

83
00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.759
is essentially shadow maps are just like textures

84
00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:39.719
and can have mismatching sampling rates.

85
00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:43.719
So if you have more screen pixels than shadow pixels,

86
00:03:43.719 --> 00:03:46.879
you'll get aliasing.

87
00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:49.159
As well as more shadow pixels than screen pixels,

88
00:03:49.159 --> 00:03:52.389
you get essentially wasted work.

89
00:03:52.389 --> 00:03:55.789
It's impossible to match these rates perfectly.

90
00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:58.310
And you also get perspective aliasing

91
00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:01.710
due to camera positions.

92
00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:05.680
Now, when it comes to virtual shadow mapping,

93
00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.759
they deal with similar issues.

94
00:04:08.759 --> 00:04:12.800
But they handle shadows similarly.

95
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:14.439
But virtual texturing will essentially

96
00:04:14.439 --> 00:04:17.639
split the texture and mipchain into tiles.

97
00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:19.519
So it only streams the tiles that it needs.

98
00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:22.040
So when I was mentioning earlier

99
00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.000
that it is recommended to actually use virtual textures,

100
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.120
this is the main reason why.

101
00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:31.319
When it comes to local lights, you get a 16 by 16K resolution

102
00:04:31.319 --> 00:04:33.189
with eight mips.

103
00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:36.670
Spotlights use a single virtual shadow map.

104
00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:39.670
Point and rectangular lights use six virtual shadow maps.

105
00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:42.540
And that would be one per cubic phase.

106
00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.139
You get 2K more than conventional shadow maps.

107
00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:50.730
And it can still run out if you make massive local lights.

108
00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:55.990
But you probably won't be encountering those issues.

109
00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:57.670
When it comes to directional lights,

110
00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.870
16K is just nowhere near enough anyways

111
00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:05.269
because they essentially work for the entirety of the world.

112
00:05:05.269 --> 00:05:09.629
So mipmaps are going to cover the same amount of work

113
00:05:09.629 --> 00:05:10.750
but half the resolution.

114
00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:14.430
And we essentially use clipmap levels that are all 16K

115
00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:15.990
but cover double the area.

116
00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:18.870
So the idea is that, depending on the location,

117
00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:22.829
we create snaps of the clipmaps

118
00:05:22.829 --> 00:05:24.910
to make sure that the pages fall on the grid.

119
00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.629
And essentially get really nice shadows

120
00:05:27.629 --> 00:05:32.389
for the entirety of the scene

121
00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:34.069
while caching stuff for a lot of fallbacks

122
00:05:34.069 --> 00:05:35.550
and things like that.

123
00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.990
So you can see the virtual shadow maps

124
00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.629
allow for more precise control.

125
00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:41.910
But let's take a look at what we can do

126
00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.470
to actually change the look of those shadows.

127
00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:46.269
So you can use some console variables

128
00:05:46.269 --> 00:05:47.870
to get better resolutions

129
00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:51.519
and better effects at the cost of performance.

130
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.180
You can use some of the commands that you see here

131
00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:57.660
in the console to actually adjust the number of rays.

132
00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.620
The fewer the rays, the greater the noise.

133
00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.220
But if you set it to zero,

134
00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:05.240
it'll just create a straight up hard shadow.

135
00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.399
And you can also change the number of samples

136
00:06:08.399 --> 00:06:10.040
that are used for shadow rays.

137
00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:15.259
And again, the higher the value, the worse performance.

138
00:06:15.459 --> 00:06:17.540
But using values between four and eight

139
00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.430
seems to work pretty well.

140
00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:22.069
So the penumbra has limits,

141
00:06:22.069 --> 00:06:23.629
but you can extend those limits again

142
00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:28.519
if you use some of the commands for the console here.

143
00:06:28.519 --> 00:06:31.199
So if you use either of these,

144
00:06:31.199 --> 00:06:32.399
you can start tweaking the values

145
00:06:32.399 --> 00:06:36.519
to essentially get more reasonable-looking shadows

146
00:06:36.519 --> 00:06:40.620
for the performance that you're looking for.

147
00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:41.740
When it comes to visualizing

148
00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:43.579
and actually being able to see

149
00:06:43.579 --> 00:06:46.410
what changes that you're doing are actually doing,

150
00:06:47.529 --> 00:06:48.610
here's some useful ones.

151
00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:49.569
So you've got the shadow mask,

152
00:06:49.569 --> 00:06:50.810
which is the final shadow mask

153
00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.449
that's being used for the shading,

154
00:06:53.449 --> 00:06:55.670
the clip map or mip level.

155
00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:58.870
So that's the chosen clip map for directional lights

156
00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.430
or mip for local lights.

157
00:07:01.779 --> 00:07:02.620
The virtual page,

158
00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:04.779
so there's the visualization of the page address

159
00:07:04.779 --> 00:07:06.100
and the cache page,

160
00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.620
which they're tinted green, essentially,

161
00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:12.540
and uncache-ed areas are tinted red.

162
00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:15.220
Pages where only the static page is cached

163
00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:16.259
will show up as blue.

164
00:07:16.259 --> 00:07:18.310
So that's something to keep in mind.

165
00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:20.670
Again, to get these visualizations,

166
00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:22.069
especially for anything that's actually

167
00:07:22.069 --> 00:07:23.310
used in virtual texturing,

168
00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.149
like clip maps and things like that,

169
00:07:25.149 --> 00:07:27.870
or these mips,

170
00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.709
you will have to turn on Shader Model 6

171
00:07:29.709 --> 00:07:31.149
or run DirectX 12,

172
00:07:31.149 --> 00:07:36.290
like we mentioned earlier on our Lumen talk.

173
00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:37.129
And that's really it.

174
00:07:37.129 --> 00:07:40.170
After this, we're just gonna load up a project

175
00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:41.649
and just create some lights,

176
00:07:41.649 --> 00:07:43.170
create some glowing objects,

177
00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:44.370
keep things nice and simple

178
00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:47.569
to make sure that we can actually

179
00:07:47.569 --> 00:07:49.649
take a look at things properly.

180
00:07:49.649 --> 00:07:50.889
But yeah, mess around,

181
00:07:50.889 --> 00:07:53.089
see if you can create some for yourself.
